#!/bin/sh
# script/interleaving-check: the hand-rolled atomic protocols under loom, which explores EVERY
# thread interleaving and every reordering the C11 memory model permits (milestone 80). See
# notes/interleaving.md.
#
# Name: provisional. Minted 2026-08-04 by milestone 80's lane. Named for the question it answers
# (which interleavings are legal) rather than for the tool that answers it (loom), following
# `script/undefined-behavior-check`, which calef ratified on 2026-08-03 for exactly that reason:
# he turned down `miri` there because "it names the tool rather than the function". So the
# principle is signed and this is its second application; what is unsigned is the noun chosen for
# the question. no architect has ratified it.
#
# The gap this fills. CLAUDE.md's fourth rule is "assume weak memory ordering", and until this
# script existed nothing in the tree could falsify a violation of it. QEMU's TCG executes guest
# atomics conservatively and explores almost none of the orderings aarch64 and riscv64 permit, so an
# acquire that should have been an acquire-release passes script/test, the CPU matrix and every CI
# leg, then fails on real silicon at a rate and a location that will not reproduce under emulation.
# Kani proves single-threaded properties and says so (notes/verification.md); Miri checks aliasing,
# not orderings; the HVF leg runs real orderings but samples them. Loom searches them.
#
# **What it covers**: `crates/work_steal_slot` (renamed from `steal_request` 2026-08-23; the
# work-steal handshake, the pilot and the kernel's one lock-free cross-core protocol),
# `crates/clock_contract` (the cross-address-space seqlock, where the survey's real bug was),
# `crates/thread_wake_handshake` (renamed from `wake_handshake` 2026-08-23; the scheduler's
# block/wake protocol, extracted after the VisionFive 2's fourth bench stop; lock-based, so what
# loom searches there is the interleaving of critical sections rather than memory orderings), and
# `crates/memory_corruption_canary_gate` (renamed from `canary_gate` 2026-08-23; the corruption
# canary's arm/check/disarm serialization, extracted after its two-flag spelling raced; the only
# retrofit so far whose bug was in the protocol itself), and `crates/memory_regions` (renamed from
# `regions` 2026-08-23; the untyped region claim, the fourth retrofit and the first on the
# memory-reclamation path, added by milestone 135 (the region claim, under loom) after a real double
# free), and `crates/spin_lock` (the lock under the kernel's `IrqSafeMutex`, whose Acquire
# compare-exchange and Release store supply most of the kernel's happens-before edges; its models
# contend two and three threads). The note carries the survey and each model's honest limits.
#
# **`crates/memory_regions` carries the falsification witness**, which is the one harness in this script
# that asserts a protocol is BROKEN: `the_pre_fix_protocol_double_frees_and_this_model_finds_it`
# reconstructs `untyped::destroy` as it stood before pull request #316 and passes only when loom
# finds the double free in it. A model that cannot fail proves nothing, and this tree's standard is
# that an assertion nobody has watched fail is not a gate (milestone 62); that harness is what keeps
# the standard met without anyone having to remember to break the code by hand.
#
# **Loom models C11, not ARM and not RISC-V.** It narrows the gap rather than closing it: a bug it
# finds is real, and a clean run is not a proof about the silicon. Milestone 81's HVF leg is the
# complementary evidence. The note says this at more length; it is the caveat to repeat.
#
# Not in script/test and not in script/ci-build's table, on purpose, for the same reason as
# script/undefined-behavior-check: this searches a state space where the host tests check a value.
#
# **CI: the `interleavings` job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs this script on every pull request
# and merge group** (milestone 746 (loom runs in CI), provisional number; before it nothing ran this script). It is
# out of `script/ci-build`'s table because the job calls the script directly. The job is a
# required status check (calef, 2026-10-04 UTC; ruleset 19596094). Measured 12.4 seconds by milestone
# 232's audit on 2026-09-03 and 23.8 seconds on 2026-10-03, both cold, for all 26 harnesses. See
# notes/check-inventory.md.
# Extra args go to `cargo test`, so `script/interleaving-check a_relaxed_pairing` runs one harness.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# --cfg loom is what swaps the crate's atomics for loom's instrumented ones AND what activates the
# `[target.'cfg(loom)'.dependencies]` table that makes loom available at all. It has to reach the
# crate under test, so it goes in RUSTFLAGS rather than on the cargo invocation.
#
# -D warnings because script/lint cannot see this code. `cfg(loom)` is set here and nowhere else, so
# every harness in the tree is invisible to the clippy gate, which is exactly the blind spot
# milestone 113 found in the Kani harnesses and built a shim to close. Here it costs one flag: this
# script compiles the harnesses anyway, so it may as well be the thing that lints them.
#
# --release because the search is compute-bound and an unoptimised loom run is minutes where an
# optimised one is seconds. Nothing here depends on debug assertions.
echo "==> loom: the hand-rolled atomic protocols, every interleaving"
RUSTFLAGS="--cfg loom -D warnings" cargo test --release \
    -p work_steal_slot -p clock_contract -p thread_wake_handshake -p memory_corruption_canary_gate \
    -p memory_regions -p spin_lock --lib "$@"
